    {"id": "susan-smith-1994", "name": "Michael & Alex Smith", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 1994, "date": "October 25, 1994", "state": "South Carolina", "city": "Union", "age": None, "gender": "Male", "summary": "Susan Smith drowned her two sons Michael (3) and Alex (14 months) by rolling her car into John D. Long Lake. She initially claimed a Black carjacker took them.", "lastSeen": "October 25, 1994, John D. Long Lake, Union, South Carolina", "tags": ["homicide", "children", "South Carolina", "1990s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Susan Smith - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susan_Smith"}, {"title": "Smith case - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2019/07/22/us/susan-smith-25-years/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On October 25, 1994, Susan Smith of Union, South Carolina reported that a Black man had carjacked her vehicle with her two sons inside. The nation rallied around the grieving mother as police launched a massive manhunt.", "Nine days later, Smith confessed to rolling her Mazda into John D. Long Lake with three-year-old Michael and fourteen-month-old Alex strapped into their car seats. She had watched the car sink.", "Smith's motive was linked to a romantic relationship with a wealthy local man who had told her he didn't want children. Her false racial accusation inflamed tensions in the small Southern town.", "Smith was convicted of two counts of murder and sentenced to life in prison with the possibility of parole after 30 years. She became eligible for parole in 2024."], "timeline": [{"date": "1994-10-25", "event": "Susan Smith drowns her two sons in John D. Long Lake."}, {"date": "1994-11-03", "event": "Smith confesses to the murders."}, {"date": "1995-07-28", "event": "Smith convicted; sentenced to life."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "andre-dillard-1988", "name": "Anthonette Cayedito", "type": "Abduction", "status": "Unsolved", "year": 1986, "date": "April 6, 1986", "state": "New Mexico", "city": "Gallup", "age": 9, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Nine-year-old Anthonette Cayedito was taken from her home in Gallup, New Mexico after answering a knock at the door late at night. A girl matching her description called 911 years later.", "lastSeen": "April 6, 1986, her home in Gallup, New Mexico", "tags": ["abduction", "child", "New Mexico", "1980s", "unsolved"], "sources": [{"title": "Disappearance of Anthonette Cayedito - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disappearance_of_Anthonette_Cayedito"}, {"title": "Anthonette Cayedito - NCMEC", "url": "https://www.missingkids.org/poster/NCMC/774548"}], "narrative": ["On April 6, 1986, nine-year-old Anthonette Christine Cayedito answered a knock at the door of her family's home in Gallup, New Mexico around 3 a.m. Her younger sister Seneca witnessed a man and woman grab Anthonette and force her into a brown van.", "Anthonette's mother Penny was asleep at the time and did not realize her daughter was missing until later. Questions about the household circumstances delayed the initial investigation.", "In 1991, a girl called 911 from a pay phone in Carson City, Nevada and said 'I'm Anthonette Cayedito' before being cut off. In a separate incident, a waitress at a restaurant reported a girl writing 'help me' on a napkin who matched Anthonette's description.", "Despite these leads, Anthonette has never been found. The case remains one of New Mexico's most prominent cold cases."], "timeline": [{"date": "1986-04-06", "event": "Anthonette Cayedito taken from her home in Gallup."}, {"date": "1991-01-01", "event": "Girl claiming to be Anthonette calls 911 from Nevada."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "andrea-yates-2001", "name": "Yates Children", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2001, "date": "June 20, 2001", "state": "Texas", "city": "Houston", "age": None, "gender": "Mixed", "summary": "Andrea Yates drowned her five children in the bathtub during a severe psychotic episode. She was found not guilty by reason of insanity after an initial conviction was overturned.", "lastSeen": "June 20, 2001, family home in Clear Lake City, Houston, Texas", "tags": ["homicide", "children", "Texas", "2000s", "mental illness", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Andrea Yates - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrea_Yates"}, {"title": "Yates case - Houston Chronicle", "url": "https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/andrea-yates/"}], "narrative": ["On June 20, 2001, Andrea Yates systematically drowned all five of her children—Noah (7), John (5), Paul (3), Luke (2), and Mary (6 months)—in the bathtub of their Clear Lake City home in Houston, Texas, while her husband Rusty was at work.", "Andrea then called 911 and told the operator 'I just killed my kids.' She had a long history of severe mental illness, including postpartum psychosis, and had been hospitalized multiple times. She believed Satan was inside her and that killing the children would save their souls.", "She was convicted of capital murder in 2002, but the conviction was overturned in 2005 after a prosecution expert witness was found to have given false testimony. In a second trial in 2006, she was found not guilty by reason of insanity.", "The case sparked national debate about postpartum mental illness, the insanity defense, and the responsibility of family members and medical professionals in preventing tragedy."], "timeline": [{"date": "2001-06-20", "event": "Andrea Yates drowns her five children."}, {"date": "2002-03-12", "event": "Yates convicted of capital murder."}, {"date": "2006-07-26", "event": "Found not guilty by reason of insanity in retrial."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "kendrick-johnson-2013", "name": "Kendrick Johnson", "type": "Suspicious Death", "status": "Unsolved", "year": 2013, "date": "January 10, 2013", "state": "Georgia", "city": "Valdosta", "age": 17, "gender": "Male", "summary": "Seventeen-year-old Kendrick Johnson was found dead inside a rolled-up gym mat at his high school in Valdosta, Georgia. Officials ruled it accidental; his family alleges murder.", "lastSeen": "January 10, 2013, Lowndes High School, Valdosta, Georgia", "tags": ["suspicious death", "Georgia", "2010s", "unsolved"], "sources": [{"title": "Death of Kendrick Johnson - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Kendrick_Johnson"}, {"title": "Kendrick Johnson case - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2013/10/09/us/georgia-gym-mat-death/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On January 11, 2013, seventeen-year-old Kendrick Johnson was found dead inside a vertically stored rolled-up wrestling mat in the gymnasium of Lowndes High School in Valdosta, Georgia. He had been missing since the previous day.", "The Lowndes County Sheriff's Office ruled his death accidental, theorizing he had reached into the mat to retrieve a shoe and became stuck upside down. The Georgia Bureau of Investigation concurred.", "Kendrick's family disputed the finding, noting injuries to his face and the improbability of the accident scenario. They commissioned independent autopsies that found blunt force trauma to the right neck and jaw area. The family alleged a cover-up.", "After years of advocacy, the DOJ investigated but closed the case without charges in 2016. In 2021, a sheriff reopened the case. Kendrick's parents continued fighting for answers, believing their son was murdered."], "timeline": [{"date": "2013-01-11", "event": "Kendrick Johnson found dead in a gym mat."}, {"date": "2013-05-02", "event": "Death ruled accidental; family disputes finding."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "teresa-sievers-2015", "name": "Teresa Sievers", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2015, "date": "June 28, 2015", "state": "Florida", "city": "Bonita Springs", "age": 46, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Dr. Teresa Sievers was bludgeoned to death in her Bonita Springs, Florida home. Her husband Mark hired two friends to murder her. All three were convicted.", "lastSeen": "June 28, 2015, her home in Bonita Springs, Florida", "tags": ["homicide", "Florida", "2010s", "murder for hire", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murder of Teresa Sievers - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Teresa_Sievers"}, {"title": "Sievers case - Fort Myers News-Press", "url": "https://www.news-press.com/story/news/crime/teresa-sievers/"}, {"title": "Mark Sievers conviction - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2019/12/04/us/mark-sievers-guilty-wife-murder/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On June 28, 2015, Dr. Teresa Sievers, a holistic medicine practitioner, was found bludgeoned to death with a hammer in the kitchen of her Bonita Springs, Florida home. She had just returned from a trip with her two daughters.", "The investigation revealed that her husband Mark Sievers had orchestrated the murder, hiring his friends Curtis Wayne Wright Jr. and Jimmy Ray Rodgers to carry out the killing while he established an alibi by being out of state with the children.", "Wright turned state's evidence and testified that Mark had planned the murder for months, motivated by marital problems and a desire to collect life insurance. Rodgers, who wielded the hammer, was convicted first.", "Mark Sievers was convicted of first-degree murder in 2019 and sentenced to death. Rodgers was convicted and sentenced to life. Wright received a 25-year sentence for his cooperation."], "timeline": [{"date": "2015-06-28", "event": "Dr. Teresa Sievers found murdered in her home."}, {"date": "2019-12-04", "event": "Mark Sievers convicted; sentenced to death."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "garrett-phillips-2011", "name": "Garrett Phillips", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Unsolved", "year": 2011, "date": "October 24, 2011", "state": "New York", "city": "Potsdam", "age": 12, "gender": "Male", "summary": "Twelve-year-old Garrett Phillips was strangled in his Potsdam, New York apartment. Soccer coach Oral 'Nick' Hillary was charged but acquitted. The case remains unsolved.", "lastSeen": "October 24, 2011, his apartment in Potsdam, New York", "tags": ["homicide", "child", "New York", "2010s", "unsolved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murder of Garrett Phillips - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Garrett_Phillips"}, {"title": "Who Killed Garrett Phillips? - HBO", "url": "https://www.hbo.com/who-killed-garrett-phillips"}, {"title": "Nick Hillary acquittal - WWNY", "url": "https://www.wwnytv.com/story/nick-hillary-acquittal/"}], "narrative": ["On October 24, 2011, twelve-year-old Garrett Phillips was found unresponsive in his apartment in Potsdam, a small college town in northern New York. He had been strangled and his apartment showed signs of forced entry. He was pronounced dead at the hospital.", "The investigation focused on Oral 'Nick' Hillary, a Clarkson University soccer coach who had previously dated Garrett's mother Tandy Cyrus. Hillary, who is Black, maintained his innocence.", "Hillary was charged with second-degree murder in 2014. His trial, which included questions about racial bias in the investigation, ended in acquittal in 2016. The judge found the prosecution's evidence insufficient.", "The case remains officially unsolved. It was the subject of a 2019 HBO documentary that examined issues of race and justice in the small, predominantly white community."], "timeline": [{"date": "2011-10-24", "event": "Garrett Phillips found strangled in his Potsdam apartment."}, {"date": "2016-09-28", "event": "Nick Hillary acquitted; case remains unsolved."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "tamla-horsford-2018", "name": "Tamla Horsford", "type": "Suspicious Death", "status": "Unsolved", "year": 2018, "date": "November 4, 2018", "state": "Georgia", "city": "Cumming", "age": 40, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Tamla Horsford, a Black mother of five, was found dead at a sleepover party at a white friend's home in Cumming, Georgia. Her death was ruled accidental but her family disputes this.", "lastSeen": "November 4, 2018, a residence in Cumming, Forsyth County, Georgia", "tags": ["suspicious death", "Georgia", "2010s", "unsolved"], "sources": [{"title": "Death of Tamla Horsford - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Tamla_Horsford"}, {"title": "Tamla Horsford case - Atlanta Journal-Constitution", "url": "https://www.ajc.com/news/local/tamla-horsford/"}, {"title": "GBI investigation - WSB-TV", "url": "https://www.wsbtv.com/news/local/forsyth-county/tamla-horsford-investigation/"}], "narrative": ["On November 4, 2018, forty-year-old Tamla Horsford, a Black mother of five, was found dead in the backyard of a home in Cumming, Georgia, following an adult sleepover party attended by approximately a dozen women—Tamla was the only Black attendee.", "The Forsyth County Sheriff's Office quickly ruled her death accidental, concluding she had fallen from a second-floor balcony while intoxicated. Her blood alcohol level was elevated.", "Tamla's family disputed the finding, citing injuries inconsistent with a simple fall and noting the speed with which the case was closed. They alleged that Tamla's injuries more closely resembled those from a physical assault.", "After public pressure, the Georgia Bureau of Investigation reviewed the case in 2020 but upheld the accidental death ruling. The case became a symbol of how deaths of Black individuals in predominantly white communities can receive insufficient scrutiny."], "timeline": [{"date": "2018-11-04", "event": "Tamla Horsford found dead at a party in Cumming, Georgia."}, {"date": "2018-11-05", "event": "Death ruled accidental; family disputes finding."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "emma-walker-2016", "name": "Emma Walker", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2016, "date": "November 21, 2016", "state": "Tennessee", "city": "Knoxville", "age": 16, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Sixteen-year-old Emma Walker was shot through her bedroom wall by her obsessive ex-boyfriend William Riley Gaul while she slept. Gaul, a high school football player, was convicted.", "lastSeen": "November 21, 2016, her home in Knoxville, Tennessee", "tags": ["homicide", "child", "Tennessee", "2010s", "domestic violence", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murder of Emma Walker - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Emma_Walker"}, {"title": "Riley Gaul trial - Knoxville News Sentinel", "url": "https://www.knoxnews.com/story/news/crime/2018/riley-gaul-emma-walker-trial/"}, {"title": "Emma Walker case - Dateline NBC", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/dateline/video/emma-walker-murder"}], "narrative": ["On November 21, 2016, sixteen-year-old Emma Walker was found dead in her bed by her parents in their Knoxville, Tennessee home. She had been shot through the wall of her bedroom while she slept.", "Emma had recently broken up with William Riley Gaul, 18, a high school football player who had become increasingly obsessive and controlling. In the weeks before the murder, Gaul had engaged in a campaign of harassment, including fake kidnapping threats.", "Gaul shot through the exterior wall of Emma's bedroom with a stolen handgun in the early morning hours. He then participated in mourning and vigils for Emma before evidence linked him to the crime.", "Gaul was convicted of first-degree murder in 2018 and sentenced to life with the possibility of parole after 51 years."], "timeline": [{"date": "2016-11-21", "event": "Emma Walker shot through her bedroom wall while sleeping."}, {"date": "2018-05-09", "event": "Riley Gaul convicted; sentenced to life with parole after 51 years."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "katelyn-markham-2011", "name": "Katelyn Markham", "type": "Missing Person", "status": "Solved", "year": 2011, "date": "August 13, 2011", "state": "Ohio", "city": "Fairfield", "age": 21, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Twenty-one-year-old Katelyn Markham vanished from her Fairfield, Ohio home. Her fiancé John Carter confessed to killing her in 2022 after her remains were found.", "lastSeen": "August 13, 2011, her home in Fairfield, Butler County, Ohio", "tags": ["missing person", "homicide", "Ohio", "2010s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Disappearance of Katelyn Markham - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disappearance_of_Katelyn_Markham"}, {"title": "Katelyn Markham remains found - Cincinnati Enquirer", "url": "https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/crime/katelyn-markham/"}, {"title": "John Carter arrest - WLWT", "url": "https://www.wlwt.com/article/katelyn-markham-fiance-arrest/"}], "narrative": ["On August 13, 2011, twenty-one-year-old Katelyn Markham, a talented artist, was last seen at her home in Fairfield, Ohio by her fiancé John Carter. He reported her missing the next morning when she failed to answer texts.", "The investigation stalled for years. Carter maintained his innocence and participated in public searches and vigils for Katelyn.", "In April 2022, Katelyn's skeletal remains were found in a wooded area of Cedar Grove, Indiana, about 25 miles from Fairfield. John Carter was arrested and charged with aggravated murder.", "Carter pleaded guilty to murder and abuse of a corpse in 2023 and was sentenced to 15 years to life in prison."], "timeline": [{"date": "2011-08-13", "event": "Katelyn Markham last seen at her Fairfield home."}, {"date": "2022-04-07", "event": "Katelyn's remains found in Indiana."}, {"date": "2023-05-24", "event": "John Carter pleads guilty; sentenced to 15 years to life."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "tammy-daybell-2019", "name": "Tammy Daybell", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2019, "date": "October 19, 2019", "state": "Idaho", "city": "Salem", "age": 49, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Tammy Daybell was murdered by her husband Chad Daybell, who wanted to marry Lori Vallow. Her death was initially ruled natural but later reclassified as homicide.", "lastSeen": "October 19, 2019, her home in Salem, Idaho", "tags": ["homicide", "Idaho", "2010s", "cult", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Chad Daybell case - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chad_Daybell"}, {"title": "Tammy Daybell murder - East Idaho News", "url": "https://www.eastidahonews.com/2020/chad-daybell-tammy/"}, {"title": "Daybell trial - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2024/05/30/us/chad-daybell-sentence-death/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On October 19, 2019, Tammy Daybell, 49, died in her sleep at her home in Salem, Idaho. Her husband Chad Daybell told authorities she had died of natural causes. Despite being relatively young and healthy, her death was not immediately investigated.", "Just two weeks later, Chad married Lori Vallow in Hawaii. The speed of the marriage raised suspicions among Tammy's family and friends.", "When Lori's children Tylee Ryan and JJ Vallow were reported missing, investigators reexamined Tammy's death. Her body was exhumed in December 2019, and the cause of death was changed to homicide—she had been asphyxiated.", "Chad Daybell was charged with Tammy's murder along with the murders of Tylee and JJ. He was convicted of all three murders and sentenced to death in 2024."], "timeline": [{"date": "2019-10-19", "event": "Tammy Daybell dies; initially ruled natural causes."}, {"date": "2019-12-11", "event": "Body exhumed; death reclassified as homicide."}, {"date": "2024-06-01", "event": "Chad Daybell convicted of Tammy's murder; sentenced to death."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "alex-murdaugh-2021", "name": "Maggie & Paul Murdaugh", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2021, "date": "June 7, 2021", "state": "South Carolina", "city": "Islandton", "age": None, "gender": "Mixed", "summary": "Alex Murdaugh, a prominent South Carolina attorney, murdered his wife Maggie and son Paul at their family hunting estate to cover up financial crimes. He was sentenced to life.", "lastSeen": "June 7, 2021, Moselle estate, Islandton, South Carolina", "tags": ["homicide", "South Carolina", "2020s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murdaugh murders - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murdaugh_murders"}, {"title": "Alex Murdaugh trial - New York Times", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/news-event/alex-murdaugh-murders-trial"}, {"title": "Murdaugh case - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2022/07/14/us/alex-murdaugh-timeline/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On the evening of June 7, 2021, Alex Murdaugh called 911 to report finding his wife Maggie (52) and son Paul (22) shot dead at the family's hunting estate, Moselle, in rural Islandton, South Carolina. Maggie had been shot multiple times with a rifle, and Paul had been shot twice with a shotgun.", "Alex, a fourth-generation attorney from one of the most powerful legal families in the South Carolina Lowcountry, initially denied being at the scene. However, a video from Paul's phone placed Alex at the dog kennels near the crime scene minutes before the murders.", "The investigation unraveled a massive web of financial crimes. Alex had been stealing millions from clients, his law firm, and his family for years. Investigators theorized he killed Maggie and Paul to generate sympathy and divert attention from his collapsing financial scheme.", "Alex Murdaugh was convicted of two counts of murder in March 2023 and sentenced to two consecutive life terms without parole. He was subsequently convicted of numerous financial crimes and sentenced to 27 years for those offenses."], "timeline": [{"date": "2021-06-07", "event": "Maggie and Paul Murdaugh found shot dead at Moselle estate."}, {"date": "2023-03-02", "event": "Alex Murdaugh convicted of double murder."}, {"date": "2023-03-03", "event": "Sentenced to two consecutive life terms without parole."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "jennifer-pan-parents-2010", "name": "Sherri Papini", "type": "Hoax", "status": "Solved", "year": 2016, "date": "November 2, 2016", "state": "California", "city": "Redding", "age": 34, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Sherri Papini faked her own kidnapping in Redding, California, claiming two Hispanic women abducted her. She was found alive after 22 days. In 2022, she pleaded guilty to hoax charges.", "lastSeen": "November 2, 2016, jogging near her home in Redding, California", "tags": ["hoax", "California", "2010s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Kidnapping hoax of Sherri Papini - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kidnapping_hoax_of_Sherri_Papini"}, {"title": "Sherri Papini case - Sacramento Bee", "url": "https://www.sacbee.com/news/local/crime/sherri-papini/"}, {"title": "Papini guilty plea - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/18/us/sherri-papini-guilty-plea/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On November 2, 2016, Sherri Papini, a 34-year-old mother of two, disappeared while jogging near her home in Redding, California. Her phone and earbuds were found on the road. Twenty-two days later, she was found alive on Interstate 5, bound, branded, beaten, and with her hair cut off.", "Papini told police she had been kidnapped by two armed Hispanic women who held her captive. The case drew massive media attention and generated community fundraisers. Her husband Keith publicly advocated for her.", "In 2022, DNA evidence and cell phone data revealed that Papini had actually been staying with an ex-boyfriend in Costa Mesa, California for the 22 days. She had self-inflicted her injuries and fabricated the kidnapping story.", "Papini pleaded guilty to making false statements to a federal law enforcement officer and mail fraud (for collecting money from a victim's compensation fund). She was sentenced to 18 months in federal prison."], "timeline": [{"date": "2016-11-02", "event": "Sherri Papini 'disappears' while jogging in Redding."}, {"date": "2016-11-24", "event": "Papini found alive; claims kidnapping by two women."}, {"date": "2022-04-18", "event": "Papini pleads guilty to hoax charges; sentenced to 18 months."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "brian-walshe-2023", "name": "Ana Walshe (Brian Walshe case)", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2023, "date": "January 1, 2023", "state": "Massachusetts", "city": "Cohasset", "age": 39, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Ana Walshe vanished from her Cohasset, Massachusetts home on New Year's Day. Her husband Brian was caught on camera buying cleaning supplies and was charged with murder.", "lastSeen": "January 1, 2023, her home in Cohasset, Massachusetts", "tags": ["homicide", "Massachusetts", "2020s", "domestic violence", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Disappearance of Ana Walshe - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disappearance_of_Ana_Walshe"}, {"title": "Brian Walshe case - Boston Globe", "url": "https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/ana-walshe/"}, {"title": "Walshe arrest - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2023/01/09/us/ana-walshe-missing-massachusetts/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On January 4, 2023, Ana Walshe, 39, was reported missing from her home in Cohasset, Massachusetts. Her husband Brian initially claimed she had taken a ride-share to the airport for a work trip on January 1, but no evidence of this trip existed.", "Surveillance footage showed Brian purchasing cleaning supplies, including mops, tarps, and trash bags, at a Home Goods store. Investigators found blood and a damaged knife in the basement of the couple's home.", "Human remains—blood and body parts—were found in trash bags at a transfer station in Peabody, Massachusetts. DNA confirmed they belonged to Ana. Brian's internet search history revealed searches for 'how to dispose of a body' and 'dismemberment.'", "Brian Walshe was charged with murder and misleading investigators. He pleaded not guilty. The case remains pending trial as of 2024."], "timeline": [{"date": "2023-01-01", "event": "Ana Walshe last seen alive."}, {"date": "2023-01-04", "event": "Ana reported missing."}, {"date": "2023-01-18", "event": "Brian Walshe charged with murder."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "chad-daybell-case", "name": "Summer Wells", "type": "Missing Person", "status": "Unsolved", "year": 2021, "date": "June 15, 2021", "state": "Tennessee", "city": "Rogersville", "age": 5, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Five-year-old Summer Wells vanished from her family's property in rural Hawkins County, Tennessee. Despite massive searches of the rugged terrain, she has never been found.", "lastSeen": "June 15, 2021, her family's property in Ben Hill community, Hawkins County, Tennessee", "tags": ["missing person", "child", "Tennessee", "2020s", "unsolved"], "sources": [{"title": "Disappearance of Summer Wells - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disappearance_of_Summer_Wells"}, {"title": "Summer Wells case - WJHL", "url": "https://www.wjhl.com/news/local/summer-wells/"}, {"title": "TBI investigation", "url": "https://www.tn.gov/tbi/summer-wells.html"}], "narrative": ["On June 15, 2021, five-year-old Summer Moon-Utah Wells was last seen playing in the yard of her family's rural property in the Ben Hill community of Hawkins County, Tennessee. Her mother reported that Summer had been planting flowers in the yard before she vanished.", "An immediate search of the heavily wooded, mountainous terrain surrounding the property was launched. Hundreds of searchers, including TBI agents, FBI, and volunteer teams, combed the area for weeks.", "The case has been complicated by conflicting statements from family members and the family's distrust of law enforcement. Summer's parents have been named as persons of interest but not charged.", "Summer Wells has never been found. The Tennessee Bureau of Investigation continues to follow leads, and the case remains one of the state's most active missing child investigations."], "timeline": [{"date": "2021-06-15", "event": "Summer Wells vanishes from her family's property."}, {"date": "2021-06-16", "event": "Massive search of surrounding terrain begins."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "harmony-montgomery-2019", "name": "Harmony Montgomery", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2019, "date": "December 7, 2019", "state": "New Hampshire", "city": "Manchester", "age": 5, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Five-year-old Harmony Montgomery was murdered by her father Adam Montgomery in Manchester, New Hampshire. She wasn't reported missing until 2021 due to systemic failures.", "lastSeen": "December 7, 2019, Manchester, New Hampshire", "tags": ["homicide", "child", "New Hampshire", "2010s", "child welfare failure", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murder of Harmony Montgomery - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Harmony_Montgomery"}, {"title": "Harmony Montgomery case - Union Leader", "url": "https://www.unionleader.com/news/crime/harmony-montgomery/"}, {"title": "Adam Montgomery conviction - WMUR", "url": "https://www.wmur.com/article/adam-montgomery-harmony-trial/"}], "narrative": ["Harmony Montgomery was a five-year-old girl in Manchester, New Hampshire who was last seen alive on December 7, 2019. Her disappearance was not reported to police until December 2021—a full two years later—when the state's Division for Children, Youth and Families realized no one could account for the child.", "The investigation revealed catastrophic failures in the child welfare system. Harmony had been in foster care in Massachusetts before being placed with her biological father, Adam Montgomery, in New Hampshire. Multiple agencies lost track of her.", "Adam Montgomery was arrested in January 2022 on charges of assault and welfare fraud. As the investigation continued, Harmony's remains were eventually located. Adam was charged with second-degree murder.", "Montgomery was convicted of second-degree murder in 2024 and sentenced to 45 years to life. The case prompted major reforms in both New Hampshire and Massachusetts child welfare systems."], "timeline": [{"date": "2019-12-07", "event": "Harmony Montgomery last seen alive."}, {"date": "2021-12-27", "event": "DCYF realizes Harmony is missing; police notified."}, {"date": "2024-02-22", "event": "Adam Montgomery convicted of murder; sentenced to 45 years to life."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "chad-doerman-2023", "name": "Doerman Children", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2023, "date": "June 15, 2023", "state": "Ohio", "city": "Clermont County", "age": None, "gender": "Male", "summary": "Chad Doerman shot and killed his three young sons execution-style in the front yard of their Clermont County, Ohio home. He was sentenced to death.", "lastSeen": "June 15, 2023, Clermont County, Ohio", "tags": ["homicide", "children", "Ohio", "2020s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Chad Doerman case - Cincinnati Enquirer", "url": "https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/crime/chad-doerman/"}, {"title": "Doerman sentencing - WLWT", "url": "https://www.wlwt.com/article/chad-doerman-sentence-death/"}, {"title": "Ohio filicide case - AP News", "url": "https://apnews.com/article/chad-doerman-ohio-children-killed"}], "narrative": ["On June 15, 2023, Chad Doerman, 32, called his three young sons—Chase (7), Hunter (4), and Clayton (3)—into the front yard of their home in Clermont County, Ohio. He then shot each of them execution-style with a rifle.", "Doerman's wife attempted to flee with the youngest child but Doerman took the boy and shot him. She escaped with their infant daughter. A neighbor who witnessed part of the attack called 911.", "When deputies arrived, Doerman was on the porch. He was arrested without incident. He reportedly told officers he planned the killings and specifically said 'I had to do it.' No clear motive has been established.", "Doerman pleaded guilty to three counts of aggravated murder and was sentenced to death."], "timeline": [{"date": "2023-06-15", "event": "Chad Doerman shoots and kills his three sons."}, {"date": "2024-01-01", "event": "Doerman pleads guilty; sentenced to death."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "kouri-richins-2022", "name": "Eric Richins", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2022, "date": "March 4, 2022", "state": "Utah", "city": "Kamas", "age": 39, "gender": "Male", "summary": "Kouri Richins poisoned her husband Eric with a lethal dose of fentanyl in a Moscow Mule cocktail. She then wrote a children's book about grief. She was convicted of murder.", "lastSeen": "March 4, 2022, their home in Kamas, Summit County, Utah", "tags": ["homicide", "Utah", "2020s", "poisoning", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Kouri Richins case - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kouri_Richins"}, {"title": "Richins case - Salt Lake Tribune", "url": "https://www.sltrib.com/news/2023/kouri-richins/"}, {"title": "Richins arrest - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/08/us/kouri-richins-fentanyl-husband-book/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On March 4, 2022, Eric Richins, 39, was found dead in the bedroom of his home in Kamas, Utah. His wife Kouri told police she had made him a Moscow Mule cocktail before bed and found him unresponsive in the morning.", "Toxicology revealed a lethal level of fentanyl in Eric's system—five times the fatal dose. Investigation showed Kouri had been searching how to obtain fentanyl and had a financial motive related to Eric's life insurance and real estate holdings.", "After Eric's death, Kouri published a children's book called 'Are You With Me?' about helping children cope with grief, generating income from the story of her husband's death.", "Kouri was arrested in May 2023 and charged with aggravated murder. She was convicted and faces life in prison. The case attracted attention for the audacity of writing a grief book while allegedly being the killer."], "timeline": [{"date": "2022-03-04", "event": "Eric Richins found dead; poisoned with fentanyl."}, {"date": "2023-05-08", "event": "Kouri Richins arrested and charged with aggravated murder."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "ruby-franke-2023", "name": "Ruby Franke Children", "type": "Child Abuse", "status": "Solved", "year": 2023, "date": "August 30, 2023", "state": "Utah", "city": "Ivins", "age": None, "gender": "Mixed", "summary": "YouTube parenting vlogger Ruby Franke and therapist Jodi Hildebrandt were convicted of aggravated child abuse after Ruby's emaciated, bound son escaped to a neighbor's home.", "lastSeen": "August 30, 2023, Ivins, Washington County, Utah", "tags": ["child abuse", "Utah", "2020s", "social media", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Ruby Franke case - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_Franke"}, {"title": "Franke arrest - KSL", "url": "https://www.ksl.com/article/ruby-franke-arrest/"}, {"title": "Franke conviction - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2024/02/20/us/ruby-franke-sentenced-child-abuse/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On August 30, 2023, a malnourished child with duct tape on his extremities and open wounds escaped from a home in Ivins, Utah, and ran to a neighbor's house begging for food and water. The neighbor called 911.", "The child was identified as the son of Ruby Franke, 41, a popular YouTube parenting influencer who ran the channel '8 Passengers' with over 2 million subscribers. Ruby had been living with Jodi Hildebrandt, a licensed therapist and her business partner.", "Police found a second Franke child in similar condition at the home. Both children were severely malnourished, dehydrated, and showed signs of prolonged physical abuse. They had been bound, starved, and subjected to physical punishments framed as 'repentance.'", "Ruby Franke and Jodi Hildebrandt both pleaded guilty to four counts of aggravated child abuse. Each was sentenced to one to 15 years in prison per count, with sentences running consecutively."], "timeline": [{"date": "2023-08-30", "event": "Franke child escapes; Ruby Franke and Jodi Hildebrandt arrested."}, {"date": "2024-02-20", "event": "Franke and Hildebrandt sentenced to 4-60 years each."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "idaho-student-murders-2022", "name": "Idaho Student Murders", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2022, "date": "November 13, 2022", "state": "Idaho", "city": "Moscow", "age": None, "gender": "Mixed", "summary": "Four University of Idaho students were stabbed to death in their off-campus home in Moscow, Idaho. Bryan Kohberger, a criminology PhD student, was charged with their murders.", "lastSeen": "November 13, 2022, 1122 King Road, Moscow, Idaho", "tags": ["homicide", "Idaho", "2020s", "college", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "University of Idaho killings - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Idaho_killings"}, {"title": "Idaho murders - New York Times", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/news-event/idaho-student-killings"}, {"title": "Bryan Kohberger arrest - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2022/12/30/us/bryan-kohberger-arrest-idaho-college-killings/index.html"}], "narrative": ["In the early morning hours of November 13, 2022, four University of Idaho students were stabbed to death in their off-campus rental home at 1122 King Road in Moscow, Idaho. The victims were Kaylee Goncalves (21), Madison Mogen (21), Xana Kernodle (20), and Ethan Chapin (20).", "Two other roommates were home at the time but were unharmed and did not realize what had happened until later that morning. The attack appeared targeted, though all four victims were killed.", "Six weeks later, on December 30, 2022, Bryan Kohberger, a 28-year-old PhD student in criminology at Washington State University (located just 8 miles away), was arrested in Pennsylvania. He was linked through DNA evidence found on a knife sheath left at the scene and cell phone location data.", "Kohberger was charged with four counts of first-degree murder and one count of burglary. His trial is scheduled for 2025. The case generated intense social media speculation and raised questions about online sleuthing."], "timeline": [{"date": "2022-11-13", "event": "Four University of Idaho students stabbed to death."}, {"date": "2022-12-30", "event": "Bryan Kohberger arrested in Pennsylvania."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "eliza-fletcher-2022", "name": "Eliza Fletcher", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2022, "date": "September 2, 2022", "state": "Tennessee", "city": "Memphis", "age": 34, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Teacher and heiress Eliza Fletcher was abducted while jogging near the University of Memphis and murdered. Cleotha Abston, who had a prior kidnapping conviction, was sentenced to life.", "lastSeen": "September 2, 2022, jogging near Central Avenue, Memphis, Tennessee", "tags": ["homicide", "Tennessee", "2020s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murder of Eliza Fletcher - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Eliza_Fletcher"}, {"title": "Eliza Fletcher case - Commercial Appeal", "url": "https://www.commercialappeal.com/story/news/crime/eliza-fletcher/"}, {"title": "Cleotha Abston conviction - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2022/09/05/us/eliza-fletcher-memphis-missing/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On September 2, 2022, Eliza Fletcher, a 34-year-old junior kindergarten teacher and granddaughter of a Memphis billionaire, was abducted while jogging near the University of Memphis campus around 4:30 a.m. Surveillance footage showed a man approaching her and forcing her into a dark SUV.", "The suspect was identified as Cleotha Abston (later known as Cleotha Henderson), 38, who had previously served 20 years in prison for kidnapping a prominent Memphis attorney in 2000. DNA evidence from a pair of sandals found near the abduction site matched Abston.", "Eliza's body was found on September 5 behind a vacant duplex in South Memphis. She had been struck in the head and asphyxiated. Abston was arrested the same day her body was found.", "Abston pleaded guilty to first-degree murder, kidnapping, and rape in 2024 and was sentenced to life in prison without the possibility of parole. The case reignited debates about the parole system and women's safety while exercising."], "timeline": [{"date": "2022-09-02", "event": "Eliza Fletcher abducted while jogging in Memphis."}, {"date": "2022-09-05", "event": "Her body found; Cleotha Abston arrested."}, {"date": "2024-02-06", "event": "Abston pleads guilty; sentenced to life without parole."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "brian-flores-case-2023", "name": "Gilgo Beach (additional victims)", "type": "Serial Killer Victims", "status": "Solved", "year": 2024, "date": "January 16, 2024", "state": "New York", "city": "Gilgo Beach", "age": None, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Rex Heuermann was charged with additional Gilgo Beach murders as DNA evidence continued to mount. The investigation expanded to potentially 10+ victims.", "lastSeen": "Various dates, Long Island, New York", "tags": ["serial killer", "New York", "2020s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Gilgo Beach investigation - NBC News", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/gilgo-beach-murders-rex-heuermann-additional-charges"}, {"title": "Heuermann additional charges - Newsday", "url": "https://www.newsday.com/long-island/crime/gilgo-beach-murders-heuermann/"}, {"title": "Suffolk County DA - Gilgo Beach", "url": "https://suffolkcountyny.gov/da/gilgo-beach-investigation"}], "narrative": ["In January 2024, Rex Heuermann was charged with two additional Gilgo Beach murders beyond the initial four charges from July 2023, bringing the total to six murder counts. The victims were Jessica Taylor and Sandra Costilla.", "The investigation continued to uncover evidence connecting Heuermann to additional victims. Investigators used genealogical DNA, digital evidence, and Heuermann's own cell phone and internet history to build their case.", "Evidence showed Heuermann had used burner phones to contact victims and had researched methods of avoiding detection. His wife and daughters were reportedly unaware of his activities.", "The case continued to expand in 2024, with investigators examining whether Heuermann may be responsible for additional unsolved homicides along Long Island's barrier beaches. He pleaded not guilty to all charges."], "timeline": [{"date": "2024-01-16", "event": "Heuermann charged with two additional Gilgo Beach murders."}, {"date": "2024-06-01", "event": "Investigation expands; more charges possible."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "vicky-white-casey-white-2022", "name": "Vicky White & Casey White Escape", "type": "Fugitive", "status": "Solved", "year": 2022, "date": "April 29, 2022", "state": "Alabama", "city": "Florence", "age": None, "gender": "Mixed", "summary": "Corrections officer Vicky White helped murder suspect Casey White escape from an Alabama jail. They were fugitives for 11 days before being caught in Indiana. Vicky died from a self-inflicted gunshot.", "lastSeen": "April 29, 2022, Lauderdale County Jail, Florence, Alabama", "tags": ["fugitive", "Alabama", "2020s", "law enforcement", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Escape of Casey White - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escape_of_Casey_White"}, {"title": "Vicky White case - AL.com", "url": "https://www.al.com/news/huntsville/casey-white-vicky-white/"}, {"title": "Manhunt ends - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/09/us/casey-white-vicky-white-captured/index.html"}], "narrative": ["On April 29, 2022, Lauderdale County Assistant Director of Corrections Vicky White, 56, staged an escape for murder suspect Casey White, 38 (no relation), from the county jail in Florence, Alabama. She told colleagues she was taking Casey to a mental health evaluation that didn't exist.", "Vicky had secretly been in a romantic relationship with Casey, who was awaiting trial for capital murder. She sold her home, bought a getaway car, and planned the escape meticulously. She submitted her retirement paperwork the day of the escape.", "A nationwide manhunt ensued for 11 days. The fugitives were tracked to Evansville, Indiana, where U.S. Marshals located them at a motel. During the pursuit, Vicky shot herself in the head. She died from her injuries.", "Casey White was recaptured and returned to Alabama, where he was convicted of the original capital murder charge and the escape. He was sentenced to life without parole."], "timeline": [{"date": "2022-04-29", "event": "Vicky White helps Casey White escape from jail."}, {"date": "2022-05-09", "event": "Captured in Indiana; Vicky dies from self-inflicted gunshot."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "brian-kohberger-idaho", "name": "Bryan Kohberger (additional details)", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Pending Trial", "year": 2022, "date": "November 13, 2022", "state": "Idaho", "city": "Moscow", "age": 28, "gender": "Male", "summary": "Criminology PhD student Bryan Kohberger was charged with the murders of four University of Idaho students. His trial is expected in 2025. He has maintained his innocence.", "lastSeen": "November 13, 2022, Moscow, Idaho", "tags": ["homicide", "Idaho", "2020s", "pending trial"], "sources": [{"title": "Bryan Kohberger - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryan_Kohberger"}, {"title": "Kohberger case - Moscow-Pullman Daily News", "url": "https://dnews.com/local/kohberger/"}, {"title": "Idaho murder case - NBC News", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/bryan-kohberger-idaho-murders-case"}], "narrative": ["Bryan Christopher Kohberger, born in 1994 in Albrightsville, Pennsylvania, was a PhD student in criminology at Washington State University when he was arrested for the November 2022 murders of four University of Idaho students.", "Evidence against Kohberger includes DNA from a knife sheath left at the scene, cell phone data placing him near the residence in the early morning hours, and surveillance footage of his white Hyundai Elantra in the area.", "Kohberger entered a not-guilty plea and his defense has challenged the DNA evidence and cell phone tracking methods. His attorneys have indicated they intend to present an alibi defense.", "The case has been closely watched for its implications regarding genetic genealogy in criminal investigations and the role of cell phone location data as evidence."], "timeline": [{"date": "2022-11-13", "event": "Four students murdered in Moscow, Idaho."}, {"date": "2022-12-30", "event": "Kohberger arrested at his parents' home in Pennsylvania."}, {"date": "2023-05-22", "event": "Kohberger enters not-guilty plea."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "gabby-petito-foundation", "name": "Gabby Petito (foundation/legacy)", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2021, "date": "September 19, 2021", "state": "Wyoming", "city": "Spread Creek", "age": 22, "gender": "Female", "summary": "After Gabby Petito's murder, her family established the Gabby Petito Foundation to support domestic violence awareness. New legislation was passed in her name.", "lastSeen": "September 19, 2021, Bridger-Teton National Forest, Wyoming", "tags": ["homicide", "Wyoming", "2020s", "domestic violence", "legislation", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Gabby Petito Foundation", "url": "https://www.gabbypetitofoundation.org/"}, {"title": "Gabby's Law - New York State", "url": "https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2023/S1105"}, {"title": "Petito case legacy - People", "url": "https://people.com/crime/gabby-petito-foundation-legacy/"}], "narrative": ["Following the murder of Gabby Petito in August 2021 and the subsequent death of her fiancé Brian Laundrie, Gabby's family channeled their grief into advocacy. They established the Gabby Petito Foundation to support domestic violence victims and fund education.", "The case highlighted the so-called 'Missing White Woman Syndrome'—the disproportionate media attention given to missing white women compared to missing persons of color. Gabby's parents acknowledged this disparity and advocated for all missing persons.", "Several states passed or proposed legislation in Gabby's name, including bills requiring law enforcement to provide domestic violence resources during welfare checks and improving interstate communication about missing persons.", "The case also led to reforms in how the National Park Service and federal lands agencies handle missing persons reports and coordinate with local law enforcement."], "timeline": [{"date": "2021-09-19", "event": "Gabby Petito's remains found in Wyoming."}, {"date": "2021-10-20", "event": "Brian Laundrie found dead with confession."}, {"date": "2022-01-01", "event": "Gabby Petito Foundation established."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "fotis-dulos-2020", "name": "Michelle Troconis", "type": "Conspiracy to Murder", "status": "Solved", "year": 2024, "date": "March 8, 2024", "state": "Connecticut", "city": "Farmington", "age": 49, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Michelle Troconis was convicted of conspiracy to commit murder in the killing of Jennifer Dulos. She helped her boyfriend Fotis Dulos dispose of evidence after he killed his wife.", "lastSeen": "Various dates, Farmington, Connecticut", "tags": ["homicide conspiracy", "Connecticut", "2020s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Michelle Troconis trial - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disappearance_of_Jennifer_Dulos"}, {"title": "Troconis conviction - Hartford Courant", "url": "https://www.courant.com/news/connecticut/troconis-verdict/"}, {"title": "Troconis sentencing - CNN", "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2024/05/31/us/michelle-troconis-sentencing-jennifer-dulos/index.html"}], "narrative": ["Michelle Troconis, 49, was the girlfriend of Fotis Dulos at the time he murdered his estranged wife Jennifer Dulos in May 2019. She was charged with conspiracy to commit murder, tampering with evidence, and hindering prosecution.", "At trial, prosecutors presented surveillance footage showing Troconis and Fotis Dulos driving through Hartford disposing of garbage bags containing Jennifer's blood and clothing. They also showed that Troconis had helped craft alibis for Fotis.", "Troconis maintained her innocence, claiming she didn't know what was in the bags and that Fotis had manipulated her. The jury rejected this defense.", "In March 2024, Troconis was convicted of conspiracy to commit murder and other charges. She was sentenced to 14.5 years in prison in May 2024."], "timeline": [{"date": "2019-05-24", "event": "Jennifer Dulos murdered; Troconis helps dispose of evidence."}, {"date": "2024-03-08", "event": "Troconis convicted of conspiracy to commit murder."}, {"date": "2024-05-31", "event": "Sentenced to 14.5 years in prison."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "chris-watts-2018", "name": "Shanann, Bella & Celeste Watts", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2018, "date": "August 13, 2018", "state": "Colorado", "city": "Frederick", "age": None, "gender": "Mixed", "summary": "Chris Watts murdered his pregnant wife Shanann and their daughters Bella (4) and Celeste (3) in Frederick, Colorado. He disposed of their bodies at his work site. He was sentenced to life.", "lastSeen": "August 13, 2018, their home in Frederick, Colorado", "tags": ["homicide", "family", "Colorado", "2010s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Watts family murders - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watts_family_murders"}, {"title": "Chris Watts case - Denver Post", "url": "https://www.denverpost.com/tag/chris-watts/"}, {"title": "American Murder - Netflix", "url": "https://www.netflix.com/title/81130609"}], "narrative": ["On August 13, 2018, Chris Watts strangled his pregnant wife Shanann (34) in their bed in their home in Frederick, Colorado. He then drove to a remote oil field work site with Shanann's body and their two daughters Bella (4) and Celeste (3), who were still alive.", "At the work site, Watts smothered Bella and Celeste and disposed of their bodies in oil tanks. He buried Shanann in a shallow grave nearby. He then reported his family missing and made emotional public pleas for their return.", "Within days, investigators identified inconsistencies in Watts's story. Surveillance footage, GPS data, and a failed polygraph led to his arrest. He initially blamed Shanann for killing the children, then confessed to all three murders.", "Watts pleaded guilty to nine charges including first-degree murder and was sentenced to five life terms without parole. The case was the subject of the 2020 Netflix documentary 'American Murder: The Family Next Door.'"], "timeline": [{"date": "2018-08-13", "event": "Chris Watts murders Shanann, Bella, and Celeste."}, {"date": "2018-08-15", "event": "Watts makes public plea; arrested hours later."}, {"date": "2018-11-06", "event": "Watts pleads guilty; sentenced to five life terms."}], "enriched": True},
    {"id": "lacey-ellen-fletcher", "name": "Orsolya Gaal", "type": "Homicide", "status": "Solved", "year": 2022, "date": "April 16, 2022", "state": "New York", "city": "New York City", "age": 51, "gender": "Female", "summary": "Orsolya Gaal was stabbed nearly 60 times in her Queens home and her body was placed in a duffel bag. Her former handyman David Bonola pleaded guilty to manslaughter.", "lastSeen": "April 16, 2022, her home in Forest Hills, Queens, New York City", "tags": ["homicide", "New York", "2020s", "solved"], "sources": [{"title": "Murder of Orsolya Gaal - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Orsolya_Gaal"}, {"title": "Orsolya Gaal case - New York Post", "url": "https://nypost.com/tag/orsolya-gaal/"}, {"title": "David Bonola plea - NBC New York", "url": "https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/local/david-bonola-orsolya-gaal-plea/"}], "narrative": ["On April 16, 2022, a trail of blood led police from a duffel bag abandoned on a sidewalk in Forest Hills, Queens to the home of Orsolya Gaal, a 51-year-old mother of two. She had been stabbed nearly 60 times inside her home.", "The duffel bag containing her body had been wheeled through the quiet residential neighborhood in the early morning hours. Security cameras captured the suspect pulling the bag behind him.", "David Bonola, 44, a handyman who had done work on the Gaal family home, was arrested days later. He had been in a sexual relationship with Orsolya and told police they had argued about ending the affair.", "Bonola pleaded guilty to first-degree manslaughter in 2023 and was sentenced to 25 years in prison."], "timeline": [{"date": "2022-04-16", "event": "Orsolya Gaal found murdered in her Queens home."}, {"date": "2022-04-21", "event": "David Bonola arrested."}, {"date": "2023-06-01", "event": "Bonola pleads guilty to manslaughter; sentenced to 25 years."}], "enriched": True},
